1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:02,619
I have found a secret map!

2
00:00:03,019 --> 00:00:05,785
Have you ever seen a glowing map?

3
00:00:06,235 --> 00:00:14,693
I have found a secret map. Have you ever seen a glowing map? Mia is sharing news and asking about experiences. This is the present perfect.

4
00:00:15,293 --> 00:00:21,848
It connects the past with now. Let's learn when to use it, how to make it, and how it is different from the past simple.

5
00:00:22,798 --> 00:00:25,920
The present perfect is a bridge between the past and now.

6
00:00:26,520 --> 00:00:29,889
Something happened in the past, and we don't say exactly when.

7
00:00:30,489 --> 00:00:35,445
What matters is the result now. I have lost my key. So I can't open the door now.

8
00:00:36,395 --> 00:00:38,064
There are three main uses.

9
00:00:38,664 --> 00:00:41,982
Life experience: I have been to Muscat three times.

10
00:00:42,582 --> 00:00:46,408
News, or a result you can see now: Whiskers has broken the cup!

11
00:00:47,008 --> 00:00:52,192
And situations that started in the past and are still true: we have lived here for six years.

12
00:00:52,792 --> 00:00:56,964
Remember: no finished time words like yesterday, or in twenty twenty.

13
00:00:58,064 --> 00:01:02,582
Look! Whiskers has found the hidden path on the map. The line has started to glow.

14
00:01:03,532 --> 00:01:06,316
The form: have or has, plus the past participle.

15
00:01:06,916 --> 00:01:10,921
With I, you, we and they, use have, or the short form, apostrophe v e.

16
00:01:11,521 --> 00:01:14,527
With he, she and it, use has, or apostrophe s.

17
00:01:15,127 --> 00:01:16,146
I've seen it.

18
00:01:16,746 --> 00:01:18,092
Mia's found a map.

19
00:01:19,042 --> 00:01:23,796
Regular verbs end in e d, the same as the past simple: play, played, played.

20
00:01:24,396 --> 00:01:28,498
But irregular verbs have their own form. Go, went, gone, or been.

21
00:01:29,098 --> 00:01:30,312
See, saw, seen.

22
00:01:30,912 --> 00:01:32,364
Eat, ate, eaten.

23
00:01:32,964 --> 00:01:34,338
Write, wrote, written.

24
00:01:34,938 --> 00:01:36,399
Find, found, found.

25
00:01:36,999 --> 00:01:40,511
This third column is the one you need. Learn it with the verb.

26
00:01:41,461 --> 00:01:43,286
A little trap: been or gone?

27
00:01:43,886 --> 00:01:46,259
She has gone to Salalah means she is there now.

28
00:01:46,859 --> 00:01:51,212
She has been to Salalah means she went there and came back. It's an experience.

29
00:01:52,162 --> 00:01:54,339
For questions, put have or has first.

30
00:01:54,939 --> 00:01:58,481
Have you ever ridden a camel? Yes, I have. Or, no, I haven't.

31
00:01:59,081 --> 00:02:01,697
Has Mia finished her homework? Yes, she has.

32
00:02:02,297 --> 00:02:04,696
How many times have you been there? Twice.

33
00:02:05,296 --> 00:02:07,426
What have you done? I've broken the cup!

34
00:02:08,326 --> 00:02:11,092
Have you ever seen a glowing map?

35
00:02:11,542 --> 00:02:13,759
For negatives, use haven't or hasn't.

36
00:02:14,359 --> 00:02:15,974
I haven't seen that film.

37
00:02:16,574 --> 00:02:17,948
She hasn't called me.

38
00:02:18,548 --> 00:02:21,515
We can also use never: we've never been to Paris.

39
00:02:22,115 --> 00:02:25,162
Never already means not, so don't say I haven't never.

40
00:02:26,062 --> 00:02:30,068
Wow... I've never seen anything like this!

41
00:02:30,518 --> 00:02:32,644
Some words love the present perfect.

42
00:02:33,244 --> 00:02:39,136
Just means a moment ago: I've just eaten. Already means sooner than expected: she's already finished.

43
00:02:39,736 --> 00:02:48,935
Yet goes at the end of questions and negatives: have you finished yet? I haven't finished yet. Ever is used in questions about life, and never in negatives.

44
00:02:49,535 --> 00:02:56,168
For is a period: for three years. Since is the starting point: since twenty twenty, since Monday, since I was six.

45
00:02:57,118 --> 00:03:01,372
When do we use the past simple instead? When we say, or know, the finished time.

46
00:03:01,972 --> 00:03:06,430
I've visited Nizwa, an experience. But: I visited Nizwa last year.

47
00:03:07,030 --> 00:03:10,065
Have you seen my bag? But: did you see it yesterday?

48
00:03:10,665 --> 00:03:13,887
She's lost her phone. But: she lost it at school on Monday.

49
00:03:14,837 --> 00:03:16,428
Now the common mistakes.

50
00:03:17,028 --> 00:03:21,511
I have seen him yesterday. Yesterday is finished, so: I saw him yesterday.

51
00:03:22,111 --> 00:03:28,484
I live here since twenty nineteen. With since, use the present perfect: I have lived here since twenty nineteen.

52
00:03:29,084 --> 00:03:32,361
She has went home. Use the participle: she has gone home.

53
00:03:32,961 --> 00:03:37,218
Did you ever eat sushi? For life experience: have you ever eaten sushi?

54
00:03:37,818 --> 00:03:41,423
I am here for two years. Say: I have been here for two years.

55
00:03:42,373 --> 00:03:45,042
Your turn to speak. Listen, then repeat.

56
00:03:45,942 --> 00:03:47,765
I've just finished my homework.

57
00:03:50,965 --> 00:03:52,681
Have you ever been to Salalah?

58
00:03:55,781 --> 00:03:57,321
She hasn't called me yet.

59
00:04:00,321 --> 00:04:02,054
We've lived here for six years.

60
00:04:05,154 --> 00:04:06,691
I've never seen snow.

61
00:04:09,541 --> 00:04:12,887
A short story. Listen for have, has, just and never.

62
00:04:13,987 --> 00:04:21,565
Mia has opened Grandma's old box. Someone has drawn a picture of a girl and a black cat. Mia has never seen it before.

63
00:04:22,465 --> 00:04:26,471
Wow... I've never seen anything like this!

64
00:04:27,071 --> 00:04:32,708
And Whiskers? Whiskers has just fallen asleep, next to the glowing map. It has been a long day.

65
00:04:33,658 --> 00:04:37,110
Quiz time. Five questions. You have five seconds each.

66
00:04:38,060 --> 00:04:40,670
Number 1. She blank her keys. She can't get in.

67
00:04:46,470 --> 00:04:48,849
Has lost. She is he, she, it, so has.

68
00:04:49,749 --> 00:04:51,958
Number 2. I blank him last week.

69
00:04:57,758 --> 00:05:00,633
Saw. Last week is finished time, so past simple.

70
00:05:01,533 --> 00:05:04,788
Number 3. We've lived here blank 2019.

71
00:05:10,588 --> 00:05:13,132
Since. Twenty nineteen is the starting point.

72
00:05:14,032 --> 00:05:16,052
Number 4. Have you finished blank?

73
00:05:21,852 --> 00:05:24,981
Yet. In questions and negatives, yet goes at the end.

74
00:05:25,881 --> 00:05:28,911
Number 5. He has blank to Salalah. He's there now.

75
00:05:34,711 --> 00:05:36,309
Gone. He is still there now.

76
00:05:37,209 --> 00:05:43,191
Let's remember. The present perfect connects the past with now: experiences, news and results.

77
00:05:43,791 --> 00:05:48,805
Have or has, plus the past participle. And learn the third form of irregular verbs.

78
00:05:49,405 --> 00:05:56,020
Use it with just, already, yet, ever, never, for and since. But with a finished time, like yesterday, use the past simple.

79
00:05:56,970 --> 00:06:02,404
Your homework: write three things you have done in your life, and two things you have never done, in the comments.

80
00:06:03,004 --> 00:06:07,931
Subscribe for more lessons, and see you next time, when we talk about the future. Bye!

